      "eyebrow": "The legal position",
      "heading": "Finding a defect is not the problem",
      "text": "Under the Road Traffic Act 1988, using a vehicle in a dangerous condition is an offence, and the duty sits with driver and operator alike. DVSA requires a documented system for defect reporting. Verbal reporting is not a system: it leaves no evidence the defect was known, assessed, or fixed. The Act does not distinguish a fleet of 3 vans from 300."
    },
    {
      "type": "steps",
      "eyebrow": "What to record",
      "heading": "Every defect report needs the same fields",
      "items": [
        { "title": "Who, when, what", "text": "Vehicle registration, date and time, driver's name, and a defect description specific enough to be unambiguous, with its location on the vehicle." },
        { "title": "Severity and photo", "text": "Is the vehicle safe to use? If not, it goes out of service. A photograph removes ambiguity about severity and is strongly recommended." },
        { "title": "Action and sign-off", "text": "Repair completed, stood down, or monitored, recorded separately and linked back, plus who authorised the return to service and when." }
      ]
    },
